rding to the structured notes, Agent 3 recommended B. We need to respond, possibly agreeing or challenging, and bring in our own facts. We have candidate facts for all candidates. We should reference the discussion (Agent 3's recommendation). We can either agree with B, or challenge and bring up other candidate strengths. We need to be concise, evidence-based.\n\nWe have own notes: Candidate A can anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, regarded as a show-off, not open to new ideas. Candidate B is very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues. Candidate C can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. Candidate D responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as a know-it-all, hot temper.\n\nWe need to discuss crew cooperation, communication, etc. We could agree with B but note concerns about nasty remarks and weak memory for numbers. Or we could argue that Candidate A's anticipation and leadership may outweigh B's communication issues. Or we could bring up Candidate D's responsibility and problem solving. But we need to keep the flow: we should respond to Agent 3's recommendation, perhaps agreeing but adding nuance.\n\nGiven the criteria, B seems strong but has communication issues. A has leadership and anticipation but is a show-off and not open to new ideas, which may affect crew cooperation. D has responsibility but hot temper and know-it-all. C has calmness and decision speed but egocentric and poor communication.\n\nWe could argue that B's communication issues (pretentious, wrong tone, nasty remarks) are serious for crew cooperation, and that A's leadership and